SCENES = [
 dict(tag='Pakistan · Foreign Ministry, Islamabad', h2='A Sheaf of Paper', hooks=['Paper does not\n{evaporate}', "This city has carried\n{other people's secrets}", 'Measuring the {distance}\nbetween two papers'], texts=[
  "A sheaf of paper. Fifteen points. Tariq Mehmood, Pakistan's assistant secretary for West Asia, counted the pages first. Seven pages of hard English. Three weeks earlier he had memorized a number that belonged to no one. Seventy-two. It evaporated before it reached Washington. This time there was paper. Paper does not evaporate.",
  "Outside, the Margalla Hills sat in morning haze. Islamabad was a city planned too well, a city that did not suit war. In the summer of 1971, Kissinger faked a stomach ache near here, vanished, and flew to Beijing. Even then, this city carried other people's secrets. Now it stood at the center of the world's most tangled war.",
  "The document was wrapped in diplomatic language. Unwrapped, its sentences were short. Put it down, and we will let you go. Tehran would not accept this paper. But a paper would come back. Measuring the distance between two papers was his job. The handover ended at ten. In his notebook he wrote only the time, not the contents."]),
 dict(tag='Langley, Virginia · CIA Iran Operations Center', h2='The Third Resignation', hooks=['Three resignation letters.\n{None saved}', 'Numbers her hand wrote\nwent to {her aunt\'s city}', '{Fordow}.\nShe typed it in herself'], texts=[
  "Leila Shirazi had written her resignation three times. The first, the day South Pars burned. The second, the dawn after Natanz was hit. The third, now, just before two in the morning. Each time she wrote it and did not save it. The cursor hovered over the button, she paused, and she closed the window. Tomorrow, then. Tomorrow was always another dawn.",
  "The fifteen points had crossed her desk too. The technical annex on the nuclear clauses: centrifuge counts, enrichment caps, inspection cycles. She had written those numbers. Sentences from her own hand were now traveling through Islamabad to Tehran, the city where her aunt lived. The document went in the name of a nation. The numbers came from one person's hand.",
  "The list of sites for inspection: Natanz, Isfahan, Arak. And Fordow. The draft from above had left Fordow out. The reason was one line: already inoperable. She typed the word in herself. Then she wrote her cable. If a counterproposal comes with the rejection, the door is not closed. Do not kill the counterproposal."]),
 dict(tag='Kuwait City · A Pharmacy in Salmiya', h2='Clouds Are White', hooks=['Fire was more frightening\nin {daylight}', 'She did not\n{charge} for the masks', '"Mom, is that a {cloud}?"\n"No. Clouds are white."'], texts=[
  "Fire was more frightening by day. At night, fire is light. By day, it is smoke. Fatima al-Rashid, a pharmacist, saw it from behind her counter. A black column rose in the southern sky, over the airport. Her customers turned toward the glass door together. No one screamed. Twenty-six days of war had taken the screaming out of people.",
  "Burning jet fuel made a different smoke, and as a pharmacist she knew what was in it. In 1991, when a retreating army set the oil wells on fire, this country had lived once under a black sky. She took boxes of masks from the shelf and stacked them on the counter. She did not charge for them. Three people already wanted inhalers. She had eleven.",
  "The radio said Pakistan had carried the American paper to Tehran. One way, a paper crossed. The other way, drones came. Her five-year-old peeked out from under the counter. \"Mom, is that a cloud?\" \"No. Clouds are white.\" Talks and bombs do not take turns. They come together, on the same day, under the same sky."]),
 dict(tag='Bahrain · Mina Salman, U.S. Fifth Fleet Liaison', h2='A Price Tag', hooks=['{15}: what America wants\n{5}: what Iran demands', 'The first answer\nhas to be {"no"}', '"This is not a rejection.\nIt\'s a {price tag}."'], texts=[
  "Captain Kim Junho laid the two papers side by side. One, a summary of the fifteen points. The other, the five conditions just read on Iranian state television. Fifteen was what America wanted. Five was what Iran demanded. The distance between them was why the ships he knew by name were still at anchor. At Panmunjom, closing such a distance took two years.",
  "Sarah Callahan came in with her notebook. \"Iran said no. So is it over, Major?\" Major Henderson set down his coffee. \"Ever seen a negotiation where the first answer was yes? The first answer has to be no, so the next sentence can start. Rejection is part of the negotiation.\" Sarah wrote it down, then looked at Kim. \"And you, Captain?\"",
  "Kim pointed to the last line: sovereignty over the Strait of Hormuz. \"This is not a rejection. It's a price tag. Iran has already been choosing which ships may pass. For the ships I count, it doesn't matter who holds sovereignty. The door just has to open.\" He smiled. It was the first time in this war Sarah had seen him smile."]),
 dict(tag='Alborz Mountains, Iran · Air Defense Command Post', h2='He Did Not Nod', hooks=['Scattered anger\nbecame {five lines}', '"Father, what did the people\nof {Kuwait} do to us?"', 'Today he\n{did not nod}'], texts=[
  "Colonel Darius Sepahvand could guess who had written the five conditions. No single person. They were the words that had crossed the command network for three weeks. We must be paid. This must never happen again. The strait is ours. Scattered anger had gathered into five lines. And once words become writing, the writing becomes history.",
  "The signals officer read out good news. Kuwait airport, fuel depot hit. A few men in the command post nodded. Darius should have nodded too. It was a professional reflex. The reflex rose to the back of his neck, and stopped. His daughter Narges's voice was there. Father, what did the people of Kuwait ever do to us? He had not answered her.",
  "In the summer of 1988, the day it accepted a ceasefire, this country called it a poisoned chalice. If these conditions were met and the war ended, how would Narges see him? As a father who won? Or as the man who nodded when Kuwait's airport burned? Today he did not nod. No one noticed. It was the only thing he could do."]),
 dict(tag='Tehran · Classroom in the Hospital Basement', h2='Cocoa', hooks=['The children called him\n{Grandpa}', 'Even now,\n{sweet things are sweet}', '"After the war,\nwill {spring be prettier}?"'], texts=[
  "Seven-year-old Nariman came carrying a paper cup in both hands. Professor Azad Moradi had been in the hospital basement for six days. He had come for his blood pressure pills and found the children of doctors and nurses in the shelter. Schools had been closed for twenty-six days. He told them stories from the Shahnameh. The children called him Grandpa.",
  "\"Grandpa, drink this if you're cold.\" The cocoa was warm. It was sweet. Even in war, cocoa was sweet. The world could change, and sweet things stayed sweet; that was what it meant for a civilization to go on. \"Today some men from Pakistan brought a paper. But our country said no. Then why did they bring it, if we were going to say no?\"",
  "\"It's how grown-ups do it. First you say no once, and then the real talking starts.\" \"That's weird.\" \"Yes. It's weird.\" After a long while she asked, \"Grandpa, when the war is over, will spring be prettier?\" Again he could not answer. Far away a siren rose and fell silent. The two of them drank their cocoa without a word."]),
 dict(tag='Langley, Virginia · CIA Iran Operations Center, Night', h2='Five Lines', hooks=['Behind five sentences\nstood {old wounds}', 'She saved it.\nThen {deleted it}', 'In five lines,\nno word of {nuclear}'], texts=[
  "Five lines arrived. Leila read them in the original Persian. An end to attacks and assassinations. Guarantees against another war. Reparations. An end to war on every front. Sovereignty over the strait. Behind each sentence stood an old wound: the coup of 1953, eight years of war, the years of sanctions. Not an enemy's history. Her aunt's country's history.",
  "She opened the resignation file. This time she pressed save. Then she deleted it at once. Not tonight. The five lines had to be analyzed. She had to find a way to keep the Islamabad channel alive. If she left, no one in this building would be left to read Fordow's pulse. Midnight passed. She began to type.",
  "Ending assassinations and guarantees against recurrence: real demands. Reparations: leverage. Sovereignty over the strait: the wording cannot bend, but in practice it can be worked out through transit rules. And what was missing: nuclear. Not one word of enrichment. The documents that end wars usually come from the hands of those who wrote the documents that started them."]),
]
